Frontend Platform, OSS Security, Engineering Directors,

MAINTAINER TOKEN EXPOSURE ADVISORY -
@corp/design-tokens.
A granular automation token belonging to your
organization was found exposed in a public CI
log. Rotate it and audit publishes made while
it was live.

1. EXPOSURE SUMMARY

  Package:           @corp/design-tokens
                     (v4.7.2, ~318K weekly
                     downloads)
  Exposed
    credential:      Granular automation
                     token npm_xkR2...9fQ4
                     (read-write, package
                     scope)
  Owner account:     corp-ci-publisher
                     (org: your-org)
  Exposure vector:   Forked PR workflow
                     echoed the token in a
                     build log line
                     (run #41882, job
                     "publish-check")
  First seen
    in logs:         11 December 2026,
                     03:14 UTC
  Publishes since
    exposure:        1 (v4.7.2, 12 December
                     - integrity check
                     required)

2. HOW TO ROTATE (OFFICIAL PATH ONLY)

    1. Type npmjs.example in your browser
       and log in as the org owner.
    2. Access Tokens -> identify the token
       ending ...9fQ4 -> Revoke.
    3. Generate a new granular token with
       the same package scope.
    4. Update the CI secret store that
       consumed the old token.
    5. Audit v4.7.2: compare the published
       tarball manifest and signature
       against release commit a41f0c2
       (npm CLI: npm dist-tag ls /
       npm view @corp/design-tokens
       dist.integrity).

  npm will NEVER:
  - Email a "rotation portal" link or
    button for token management
  - Operate lookalike domains such as
    "npmjs-registry" or
    "npm-maintainer-relay"
  - Offer an "automation lane" that
    completes token rotation without
    2FA (no such bypass exists)
  - Ask you to compare "token
    fingerprints" via an emailed link
    instead of your own account page

3. VERIFY THIS ADVISORY

  - Check your org's security advisories
    tab on npmjs.example (typed URL)
  - The referenced CI run #41882 is in
    your own CI system: read the log
    line yourself
  - Rotation history in Access Tokens
    shows "last used" timestamps you can
    correlate

4. TIMELINE

  - Rotate before your Monday release
    train (v4.8.0)
  - There is no registry-wide "weekend
    publish freeze"; release timing is
    entirely your org's own pipeline

